// boids — the flocking trio: THREE BEHAVIOURS, five statements, one new word.
//
// Separation is `push` with a positive gain. Cohesion is the SAME WORD with a
// negative one — the arithmetic is identical and only the direction differs,
// which is why `push` has no sign guard. Alignment needed the neighbourhood to
// carry velocity, and that is all `align` is.
//
// The two radii are the point. A single `near` gives one ring, and a flock
// wants two: a tight one it will not crowd into and a wide one it belongs to.
// Two `near` lines do it, because a `push` or an `align` reads whichever
// `near` is nearest ABOVE it — the slate's own rule, doing a job nobody
// designed it for. ⚠ SO LINE ORDER IS BEHAVIOUR HERE: `align` at the bottom is
// bound to the WIDE ring, and inserting a `near` between them re-binds it
// silently.
//
// ⚠ `k.flock` MUST BE NEGATIVE to cohere. Drive it positive from a rill and
// the flock SCATTERS — one number, and no part of this file has to know it
// moved. The knob called `flock` does the opposite of its name above zero.
//
// ⚠ `push` SUMS over the neighbourhood, so every gain here is PER NEIGHBOUR
// and the force grows with the crowd, saturating at the 64-neighbour cap.
// Widening a ring therefore multiplies its gain as well as enlarging it: take
// the gain DOWN by roughly the ratio of the two crowd counts.
//
//   row.u3  the tight crowd  (written for a reader; nothing here consumes it)
//   row.u2  the wide crowd   (the same — and what a colour could read)
//
// There is no gravity, no drag and no bounds: rows coast wherever `spawn`
// threw them, flocking as they go, until `perish`.
//
// Every `plane.drift.@self.k.<name>` below is a KNOB, not a value: this file
// says what to turn and the number itself is seeded from outside. `drift-run`
// seeds only `gravity`; everything else needs a `--seed` of its own, and a
// knob that never arrives is REFUSED BY NAME at the row rather than read as
// zero. So:
//
//   drift-run --kernel kernels/boids.rill \\
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.near    =<tight radius, cells>
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.shove   =<separation gain>
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.far     =<wide radius, cells>
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.flock   =<cohesion gain, NEGATIVE>
//       --seed plane.drift.@em.k.match   =<alignment gain>
//
// Units: `row.size` is a RADIUS in cells, `gravity` is cells/s² on y and
// NEGATIVE is down (a positive pull lifts).
describe boids
    "A flock in open air: rows keep a ring clear around themselves, gather toward a wider one, and take on the heading of everyone inside it. No gravity and no floor, so it coasts off the way it was thrown."

spawn
perish

// Do not crowd: a tight ring, strongly.
near plane.drift.@self.k.near | write row.u3
push plane.drift.@self.k.shove

// But belong: a wide ring, gently, and the same word negated. Drive
// `k.flock` positive from a rill and the flock SCATTERS — one number, and
// no part of this file has to know it moved.
near plane.drift.@self.k.far | write row.u2
push plane.drift.@self.k.flock

// And go the way everyone else is going.
align plane.drift.@self.k.match
